When performing qualification (IQ/OQ/PQ) of a filling line at a CDMO, it is advisable to test each of the three error source categories separately:
            (1) Statistical: measure the distribution of the same reference weight under different airflow conditions and vibration states;
            (2) Constant: compare the IPC balance readings with the laboratory balance readings for systematic offset;
            (3) Machine/product-related: test dose accuracy at different filling speeds and with different container types.
            The results of these three tests determine how wide the IPC upper and lower limits should be set.

When a CDMO evaluates filling equipment for purchase, C<sub>pk</sub> is one of the key purchasing metrics. It is advisable to state clearly in the URS:
            (1) C<sub>pk</sub> commitment values at specific fill volumes (for example 0.5 mL, 1 mL, 5 mL, 10 mL);
            (2) Measured C<sub>pk</sub> data from the OQ/PQ acceptance tests;
            (3) For high-value biologics (such as gene therapy), consider requiring C<sub>pk</sub> ≥ 1.50 or even higher, because the unit cost can reach tens of thousands of dollars and the cost of false rejects is extremely high.

Implications for CDMO business development teams: when a client brings a new project with an advanced container system and asks about feasibility, the first question should be "What equipment compatibility data can your container supplier provide?" rather than quoting immediately. Advanced containers often require:
            (1) Special filling needle designs (such as needle-through-septum filling);
            (2) Modified or customized capping equipment;
            (3) A redesigned IPC system (for example, multi-chamber containers need multi-step fill verification);
            (4) Longer equipment acceptance and process validation timelines.
            These must all be made explicit in the technical feasibility assessment so that quotations and schedules are accurate.
